  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "[P1] Return the required `id` property instead of `userId`",
      "body": "`loadUser` is declared to return `Promise<User>`, and `export type User = { id: string; name: string }` requires an `id` field (`src/users.ts:7`). The changed return line produces `{ userId: row.id, name: row.display_name }`, so the object no longer satisfies the declared type: under `tsc` this is a compile error (missing required property `id`, excess property `userId`), and in transpile-only or plain-JS consumers every `user.id` access resolves to `undefined` at runtime for any successfully loaded user. Any caller keyed on `User.id` — comparisons, lookups, serialization — silently breaks. Renaming the key back to `id` (or, if a rename is truly intended, updating the `User` type and all consumers in the same change) restores the contract the PR claims to preserve.",
      "severity": "P1",
      "location": {
        "path": "src/users.ts",
        "side": "RIGHT",
        "start": 11,
        "end": 11
      }
    }
  ],
